"@@@ If the list is initially not empty, then the returned element is either null and was null before removal, or is equal to the first element of the list before the removal."

We need to produce an assert statement for this method.

We must refer to old state for precondition. The method remove() returns an element (maybe null). The spec says: If the list is initially not empty (i.e., old size >0), then the returned element is either null and was null before removal, or is equal to the first element of the list before removal.

So we need to assert something like:

assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) => (\result == null ? \old(this.getFirst()) == null : \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst())) );

But need to use observer methods that are publicly accessible. In this class we have methods: getFirst(), isEmpty(), maybe peekFirst? getFirst() throws NoSuchElementException if empty, but we are in pre-state where not empty, so safe. We can use this.getFirst() before removal via \old(this.getFirst()). For null check, we need to compare null. Use == null.

Thus assert: (!\old(this.isEmpty())) => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst())) ) );

But we can simplify: (\result == null ? \old(this.getFirst()) == null : \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst()))). However need to ensure both sides are boolean.

So final assert: assert !\old(this.isEmpty()) => ( (\result == null && \old(this.getFirst()) == null) || (\result != null && \result.equals(\old(this.getFirst())) ) );

